    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_valid_coloring - EdgeCase\",\n    \"isPartOf\": {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_valid_coloring.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_valid_coloring()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if a coloring is valid (no adjacent nodes share a color).\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_valid_coloring()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if a coloring is valid (no adjacent nodes share a color).\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_valid_coloring()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: >-\n  Check if a coloring is valid (no adjacent nodes share a color).\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if a coloring is valid (no adjacent nodes share a color).\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L75859-L75959){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_valid_coloring(coloring)\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is valid coloring](../assets/graphs/is-valid-coloring.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"The same two-by-three ladder on both sides, each node filled with its color and badged with the color number. Left: no edge joins two nodes of the same color, so is_valid_coloring() returns True. Right: F is recolored to 2, the same color as its neighbor C, and that edge (shown in the accent color) makes is_valid_coloring() return False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA (proper) vertex coloring gives each node a color such that the two endpoints of every\nedge have different colors. This method walks over every edge and returns `False` as soon\nas it finds one whose endpoints share a color.\n\nUse it to verify a coloring that came from somewhere other than `greedy_color()`: one\nbuilt by hand, loaded from a file, produced by another tool, or modified after the fact\n(for example, after merging two color classes to reduce the number of time slots in a\nschedule).\n\nA few details of the check:\n\n- edge direction is ignored, so `A -> B` forbids `A` and `B` from sharing a color\n- self-loops are ignored (a node is never in conflict with itself)\n- nodes missing from `coloring=` are skipped, so a *partial* coloring is reported as valid\n  when none of its colored nodes conflict; it does not check that every node is colored\n- keys that are not node IDs in the graph are ignored\n:::\n\n## Parameters {.doc-parameters}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code>[coloring]{.doc-parameter-name}[:]{.doc-parameter-annotation-sep} [dict[int, int]]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   A dictionary mapping integer node IDs to integer colors, such as the output of\n    `greedy_color()`. Colors can be any integers; only equality between the colors of\n    adjacent nodes matters. Keys must be node IDs, not labels.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if no edge joins two nodes of the same color, `False` otherwise.\n:::\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~greedy_color`) \n\n:   Find a coloring in which no edge joins two nodes of the same color.\n\n[](`~is_valid_edge_coloring`) \n\n:   The same check for a coloring of the edges.\n\n[](`~chromatic_number_upper_bound`) \n\n:   The number of colors a coloring uses.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nOn a path `A`-`B`-`C`, the two ends can share a color but neighbors can't. The coloring\nmaps node IDs to colors, so it's built from the labels:\n\n::: {#e848a064 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\ngraph = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\")])\na, b, c = (graph.get_id_from_label(x) for x in [\"A\", \"B\", \"C\"])\n\nvalid = {a: 0, b: 1, c: 0}\nclash = {a: 0, b: 0, c: 1}\ngraph.is_valid_coloring(valid), graph.is_valid_coloring(clash)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nOnly colored nodes are checked, so a coloring that leaves out `C` still passes:\n\n::: {#e12a981a .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ngraph.is_valid_coloring({a: 0, b: 1})\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\nTrue\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### Checking a change to a timetable\n\n`exams()` joins two of a made-up college's courses when some student takes both, so\ntheir exams can't share a time slot. Starting from a four-slot timetable found by\n`greedy_color()`, the lecturer of Web Design asks to move its exam to slot 0. That puts\nit with Data Visualization, which shares students with it:\n\n::: {#1574ac5d .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams = ec.exams()\nslots = exams.greedy_color(strategy=\"dsatur\")\n\nweb = exams.get_id_from_label(\"Web Design\")\nmoved = {**slots, web: 0}\n\nedges = exams.get_edges_as_tuples()\nclash = [e for e, u, v in edges if moved[u] == moved[v]]\n\nlabel = exams.get_label_from_id\npairs = [(label(u), label(v)) for e, u, v in edges if e in clash]\nexams.is_valid_coloring(moved), pairs\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(False, [('Data Visualization', 'Web Design')])\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n::: {#a2e2c28e .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nexams.set_node_attrs(\"slot\", moved).plot(\n    color_by=\"slot\", highlight_edges=clash, label_by=\"label\", seed=3,\n    height=420,\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=\"ec-theme-vars\">:where(.ec-theme){--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark){.ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}}:is(html.quarto-light, body.quarto-light, html[data-bs-theme=\"light\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"true\"], body.vscode-light, body.vscode-high-contrast-light, html[theme=\"light\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#fafafa;--ect-e5dfd507:#94a3b8;--ect-8a2de1d4:#374151;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(15,23,42,0.88);--ect-21ade003:#f1f5f9;--ect-61169ea5:#c2410c;--ect-63cc1385:#d4d4d8;--ect-879b7ebc:#949497;--ect-f59ba8df:#a1a1aa}:is(html.quarto-dark, body.quarto-dark, html[data-bs-theme=\"dark\"], [data-jp-theme-light=\"false\"], body.vscode-dark, body.vscode-high-contrast:not(.vscode-high-contrast-light), html[theme=\"dark\"]) .ec-theme{--ect-3239c383:#0d1117;--ect-e5dfd507:#4b5563;--ect-8a2de1d4:#d1d5db;--ect-57425a7e:rgba(241,245,249,0.92);--ect-21ade003:#0f172a;--ect-61169ea5:#f97316;--ect-63cc1385:#3f3f46;--ect-879b7ebc:#2c2c31;--ect-f59ba8df:#71717a}</style><div cla